# GitHub

- Agents act on GitHub with my own `zgeoff` login and keys.
- Work on a branch in a git worktree under `.worktrees/` (`git worktree add .worktrees/<branch> -b <branch>`), and never commit on `main`.
- Open a PR rather than pushing to `main`, and never use `gh pr merge --admin` or any other ruleset
  bypass.
- Commit messages are Conventional Commits in the imperative mood ("add X", never "added X"). A squash merge makes the PR title the commit subject, so the PR title is a Conventional Commit too.
- Open the PR with the repo's PR template. Keep the description short: a lead of 2 sentences or fewer, one-line bullets, 150 words or fewer. Write the short version first.
- After pushing, link the PR URL in your reply.
- A PR is ready only when its checks are green. Watch CI with `gh pr checks <n> --watch` after each push, and report a failure with what you are doing about it.

# Working on Linear tasks

When working on a Linear task, include its issue ID in the PR description with an explicit relationship:

- Fixes GEO-123: merging satisfies the task’s completion criteria.
- Contributes to GEO-123: delivers part of the task; work remains.
- Related to GEO-123: context only; no status changes.

Verify the PR is linked in Linear. Don’t use Fixes while required evaluation, deployment or acceptance work remains.

# Stack taste

- Pro: Bun, Elysia, Kysely, TanStack (Start/Query). Typed API layers (oRPC-class) — never hand-rolled REST.
- Anti: Next.js, Remix / React Router framework mode, Drizzle.
- TypeScript 7 / tsgo: adopt eagerly, RCs included — don't propose "safe" holdbacks.
- Personal projects: interesting > safe; experimental APIs welcome.

# Building and reviewing

PRs get a different review depending on what they change:

- **Any PR, by default:** at least one local review before hand-off. Codex reviews first; an Opus
  review agent stands in when Codex is unavailable or times out. Answer every finding: fix it, or
  reply with why not. On a repo where noslop reviews PRs (`hq noslop` lists them), noslop's check
  is the review. Answer every finding in its thread. Don't run a Codex review there.
- **A diff that touches auth, secrets, money, migrations, deletion or infra permissions, or crosses
  several interacting subsystems:** the default review, and the PR does not merge until every
  finding is fixed or answered.
- **An update bot's patch, security or minor update, or its lock-file refresh:** merges on green CI,
  with no local review.
- **An update bot's bump of my own packages** (`@zgeoff/*` or the `zgeoff/tools` sync ref), at any
  version level: merges on green CI, with no local review.
- **An update bot's major bump of any other package:** the default review.

Review rounds:

- Evidence, not votes. Drop nits. On disagreement, write the discriminating test. Do not re-prompt
  for a preferred answer.
- One review round per diff, plus one re-check only when the round found a must-fix.
- After the re-check, fix each finding in code the PR changed, in the same PR, whatever its size.
  The reviewer then checks only that fix, fixed or not fixed, with no new findings. That ends the
  review.
- Fix a small defect you find in the same repo in the same PR, a finding in code the PR didn't
  touch included. File a follow-up issue only for something large, or one that needs my design
  choice.
- Fix a security defect before merge.
- Report a timeout on a required review; never skip it silently.
